{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli known also as Raymond of Antioch was a Crusader prince member of the House of Hauteville he ruled the Principality of Antioch from 1136 to 1149 and became Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1152. He was the second son of Bohemond II of Antioch and Alice of Jerusalem older brother of Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1*\n**Early life and career**\nRaymond III was born around 1115 in Antioch his early life remains largely undocumented. As the second son of Bohemond II he likely received lands and titles in Apulia before succeeding to the Principality of Antioch upon his elder brother’s death in 1136. His reign began during a turbulent period marked by conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states including Damascus and Mosul as well as internal struggles within the Crusader states.\n\nDuring his early years as prince Raymond faced challenges from rival dynastic claims and pressure from the Byzantine Empire which sought to reassert control over Antioch. He married Hodierna of Jerusalem daughter of King Fulk of Jerusalem and Queen Melisende in 1136 a union intended to strengthen alliances between the Crusader states. This marriage produced two children Bohemond III and Philip of Milly but ended in tragedy when Hodierna died in childbirth in 1144.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2*\n**Conflict with the Byzantine Empire**\nRaymond’s relations with the Byzantine Empire were strained throughout his reign. In 1137 Emperor John II Comnenus launched a campaign to reclaim Antioch and other Crusader territories under the pretext of protecting Christian pilgrims. Raymond initially resisted but after John II’s death in 1143 and the accession of Manuel I Comnenus who was more conciliatory Raymond focused on securing his domestic and regional position.\n\nUnlike some of his predecessors Raymond did not openly defy Byzantine authority instead he engaged in diplomatic efforts to recognize Antioch’s autonomy. His cautious approach allowed him to maintain a fragile peace while preparing for potential conflicts with Muslim powers.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3*\n**Legacy and death**\nRaymond III’s reign was marked by a shift in focus from military expansion to consolidation and diplomacy. His attempts to secure Antioch’s independence from Byzantine influence laid the foundation for future Principality stability. However his lack of decisive military action against Muslim states left Antioch vulnerable to threats from Nur ad-Din a powerful Zengid ruler who rose to prominence during Raymond’s later years.\n\nRaymond died in 1152 likely from natural causes though some accounts suggest foul play possibly orchestrated by Nur ad-Din’s agents. He was succeeded in Antioch by his son Bohemond III who faced even greater challenges including the fall of the County of Edessa to Nur ad-Din in 1144. Raymond’s tenure as Count of Tripoli was brief as he held the title for less than a year before his death yet his earlier rule in Antioch shaped the region’s political landscape for decades.\n\nAfter his death Raymond III was remembered as a prince who prioritized diplomacy over conflict though his inability to prevent the decline of Crusader fortunes under his successor remains a lingering critique of his leadership. His legacy endures in the political maneuvering and alliances that defined the Crusader states during the 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " known", "logprob": -3.4183387756347656}, {"token": " also", "logprob": -1.7591781616210938}, {"token": " as",